### What is the search insert position problem?

Given a sorted array of distinct integers and a target value, return the index of the target if it exists, or the index where it would be inserted to maintain sorted order. The solution must run in O(log n) time.

This is standard binary search with one change: instead of returning -1 on failure, you return the value of `low`.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class SearchInsertPosition {

    public static int searchInsert(int[] rates, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = rates.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (rates[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (rates[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return low;  // insertion point
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] interestRates = {200, 400, 600, 800, 1000}; // basis points
        System.out.println("Insert 500 at: " + searchInsert(interestRates, 500));
        System.out.println("Find 600 at:   " + searchInsert(interestRates, 600));
        System.out.println("Insert 100 at: " + searchInsert(interestRates, 100));
        System.out.println("Insert 1200 at: " + searchInsert(interestRates, 1200));
    }
}
```

```text
Insert 500 at: 2
Find 600 at:   2
Insert 100 at: 0
Insert 1200 at: 5
```

### Why does returning low give the correct insertion point?

The loop invariant guarantees that throughout the search, every element before `low` is strictly less than the target and every element from `high + 1` onward is strictly greater than the target. When `low` exceeds `high`, the two bounds cross and `low` points at the first element that is greater than or equal to the target. That is precisely where the target should go to preserve sorted order.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class InvariantTrace {

    public static int searchInsertWithTrace(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            System.out.println("low=" + low + " high=" + high
                + " mid=" + mid + " val=" + arr[mid]);
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        System.out.println("Loop ended: low=" + low + " high=" + high);
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500, 700, 900};
        int pos = searchInsertWithTrace(arr, 400);
        System.out.println("Insert 400 at index: " + pos);
    }
}
```

```text
low=0 high=4 mid=2 val=500
low=0 high=1 mid=0 val=100
low=1 high=1 mid=1 val=300
Loop ended: low=2 high=1
Insert 400 at index: 2
```

### How does it handle all edge cases?

The formula handles every edge case with no special code:

- Target smaller than everything: `low` never moves, returns 0 (insert at front).
- Target larger than everything: `low` advances past the end, returns `arr.length` (append).
- Target matches an element: returned early with that index.
- Empty array: `low = 0`, `high = -1`, loop never runs, returns 0.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class AllEdgeCases {

    public static int searchInsert(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return low;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {200, 400, 600, 800};
        System.out.println("Smaller than all: " + searchInsert(arr, 100));  // 0
        System.out.println("Larger than all:  " + searchInsert(arr, 1000)); // 4
        System.out.println("Exact match:      " + searchInsert(arr, 600));  // 2
        System.out.println("Between elements: " + searchInsert(arr, 500));  // 2
        System.out.println("Empty array:      " + searchInsert(new int[]{}, 5)); // 0
    }
}
```

```text
Smaller than all: 0
Larger than all:  4
Exact match:      2
Between elements: 2
Empty array:      0
```

### How is this different from standard binary search?

| Feature | Standard Binary Search | Search Insert Position |
|---|---|---|
| On miss | Returns -1 | Returns insertion index (low) |
| Use case | Check existence | Find position for insertion |
| Return value | Index or -1 | Always a valid index |
| Loop body | Identical | Identical |
| After loop | return -1 | return low |

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class StandardVsInsert {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500, 700};
        int target = 400;

        // Standard: returns -(insertion point) - 1
        int stdResult = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, target);
        int insertionPoint = -(stdResult) - 1;
        System.out.println("Standard result: " + stdResult);
        System.out.println("Insertion point: " + insertionPoint);

        // Our version: returns insertion point directly
        System.out.println("searchInsert:    " + searchInsert(arr, target));
    }

    static int searchInsert(int[] arr, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (arr[m] == target) return m;
            if (arr[m] < target) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

```text
Standard result: -3
Insertion point: 2
searchInsert:    2
```

### How is this used in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, interest-rate tier thresholds are maintained in a sorted list. When a new threshold is defined or a customer's balance needs to be classified into a tier, this routine finds where it sits so the system either reads the matching tier or inserts the new threshold while preserving sorted order.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class InterestRateTiers {

    private final List<BigDecimal> thresholds = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addThreshold(BigDecimal amount) {
        int pos = searchInsert(amount);
        if (pos < thresholds.size()
                && thresholds.get(pos).compareTo(amount) == 0) {
            System.out.println(amount + " already exists at " + pos);
        } else {
            thresholds.add(pos, amount);
            System.out.println("Inserted " + amount + " at " + pos);
        }
    }

    private int searchInsert(BigDecimal target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = thresholds.size() - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int cmp = thresholds.get(m).compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) return m;
            if (cmp < 0) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        InterestRateTiers tiers = new InterestRateTiers();
        tiers.addThreshold(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        tiers.addThreshold(new BigDecimal("100000"));
        tiers.addThreshold(new BigDecimal("75000"));
        tiers.addThreshold(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        System.out.println("Tiers: " + tiers.thresholds);
    }
}
```

```text
Inserted 50000 at 0
Inserted 100000 at 1
Inserted 75000 at 1
50000 already exists at 0
Tiers: [50000, 75000, 100000]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time | O(log n) |
| Space | O(1) |
| Best case | O(1) when mid matches |
| Worst case | O(log n) |

### How does this relate to Java's library methods?

Java's `Arrays.binarySearch` returns `-(insertion point) - 1` when the target is not found. You can extract the insertion point with `-(result) - 1`. The `searchInsert` function is a cleaner version that returns the insertion point directly.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class JavaLibrary {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500, 700};

        int found = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 300);
        System.out.println("Found 300: " + found);

        int notFound = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 400);
        int insertAt = -(notFound) - 1;
        System.out.println("Not found 400: raw=" + notFound + " insert at=" + insertAt);
    }
}
```

```text
Found 300: 1
Not found 400: raw=-3 insert at=2
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is returning `high` instead of `low` as the insertion point. When the loop ends, `high` is `low - 1`, and returning `high` gives a position one before the correct spot. Another mistake is memorizing "return low" without understanding the invariant, which makes it hard to adapt the template under interview pressure.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class ReturnHighBug {

    // WRONG: returns high instead of low
    public static int buggy(int[] arr, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (arr[m] == target) return m;
            if (arr[m] < target) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return hi; // BUG: should be lo
    }

    // CORRECT
    public static int correct(int[] arr, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (arr[m] == target) return m;
            if (arr[m] < target) lo = m + 1;
            else hi = m - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 300, 500};
        System.out.println("Buggy insert 200:   " + buggy(arr, 200));   // 0 (wrong)
        System.out.println("Correct insert 200: " + correct(arr, 200)); // 1
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy insert 200:   0
Correct insert 200: 1
```
